YANI 1: Scrolls of charging are quite hard to come by, and rightly so
since they are very handy. However, it would be cool if #dipping a
blank scroll into a potion of gain energy had a small chance of
yielding a scroll of charging. Let's say a (10-2X) out of a hundred
chance (modified +-1 by blessed/cursed scroll), where X is the number
of scrolls in the stack. This means a single scroll has roughly a 1 in
12 chance of charging, while a stack of 4 scrolls has around a 1 in 50
chance. Any stack larger than 4 is guaranteed to fail except for a
stack of 5 blessed scrolls, in which case there is a meagre 1 in a
hundred chance of getting lucky.  A failed dip results in the
scroll(s) catching fire and the potion either disappearing or
exploding violently-depending on BUC and a roll of the dice.
Yeah, it would make it easier to obtain scrolls of charging, but it
would be costly: Quite apart from the high probability of losing
scrolls and potions for no gain, the wise dungeoneer would want to
bless both scroll(s) and potion first, using up a couple of holy
waters into the bargain. Added to that the risk of explosion (which
can be painful) and I think there are sufficient negative factors to
balance the positive.

YANI 2: I think scrolls of fire should release clouds of steam when
#dipped into water (and then still go blank as normal.) It would make
them easier to identify which is probably a bad thing in terms of play
balance, but then who's going to go around dipping un-IDed scrolls on
the off chance of IDing fire? To make this more than just a cosmetic
effect and free ID, there should be a risk of summoning a hostile
steam vortex. This would make ?oF even more irksome, since they are
currently a pain in the neck only until IDed, when they become  a
rather handy source of writing paper.

YANI 3: A new type of map-tile, the steam fountain or Geyser.
represented by a # symbol, it would be a column of steam that scalds
monster, boils potions, blanks scrolls and (occasionally) spits out
steam vorticiseises. They could be generated randomly on some levels
or created by #dipping !oF into fountains/ puddles, or by (somehow)
creating a square of water on a square of lava.)


Thoughts?

